{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S10 — external integration tests cover MULTIPLE live providers\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nThe external integration suite is the only place rebar exercises a REAL provider end to end, and it\nruns on ONE provider. `.github/workflows/external-integration.yml` gates every live job on\n`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (lines 46, 124, 185, 247; the `OPENAI_API_KEY` fallback appears only in the guard\nmessage, not as a matrix arm), and `prompt-eval.yml` likewise. So the provider seam this epic built —\nfirst-class Anthropic AND Bedrock, best-effort OpenAI-compatible — has exactly one arm under live test.\nA Bedrock-only regression (throttling shape, a `cachePoint` envelope change, an inference-profile id\nthat stops resolving, structured output degrading) cannot be caught by CI today. It can only be caught\nby a human running the gates locally, which is what S7b makes possible and is NOT a substitute for\nautomation.\n\nThis is the THIRD of the three permanent provider switches, the other two being the repo config / local\nenvironment [rebar:1aa2-37cf-5cdf-48b7] and the AWS review bot [rebar:eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a]. It is\ndeliberately NOT \"flip CI to Bedrock\": the goal is live-grounded confidence across every provider we\nhold credentials for, so a MATRIX, not a switch. Flipping the single arm from Anthropic to Bedrock would\ntrade one blind spot for another.\n\n## Approach\n\nMake provider a MATRIX DIMENSION of the external suite rather than an ambient default. Run the live jobs\nonce per credentialed provider — Anthropic, Bedrock, and OpenAI where a key exists — and have each arm\nSKIP LOUDLY when its credential is absent, so a missing secret is visible as a skip rather than\nindistinguishable from a green run.\n\nSELECT THE PROVIDER THROUGH `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE`, the layered config-file pointer, rather than\nbespoke per-arm CI wiring. Each matrix arm writes a small config file setting only\n`[llm.model_classes]` and points the env var at it. This is the right instrument for three reasons:\nit is the mechanism rebar already documents for exactly this purpose (`docs/local-dev-env.md`, landed by\nS7b), it DEEP-MERGES over the discovered config instead of replacing it so the arm overrides provider\nand model and nothing else, and it means CI DOGFOODS the same override path a developer uses — so the\npointer itself gets live coverage on every run instead of being exercised only by hand. The deprecated\nbare `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is explicitly not the instrument: it cannot express per-class models.\n\nBedrock authenticates from the AWS credential chain, not an API key, so the matrix cannot key on \"an API\nkey secret exists\"; the Bedrock arm needs its own credential presence check. S7\n[rebar:9249-1569-7be9-4f67] already established scoped Bedrock access for the review-bot container —\nreuse that precedent rather than inventing a second access path.\n\nCost is the real constraint: N providers multiplies the live spend of a suite that already runs weekly.\nDecide and record whether every provider runs on every trigger, or whether non-default providers run on\na reduced cadence or a reduced case subset.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The external suite runs its live jobs for MORE THAN ONE provider, with provider as an explicit\n      matrix dimension rather than an ambient default read from whichever key happens to be set.\n- [ ] Anthropic and Bedrock are both covered. OpenAI is covered where a credential exists, or its\n      absence is recorded as a deliberate decision with the reason.\n- [ ] Each arm selects its provider via `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` pointing at a config file that sets\n      ONLY `[llm.model_classes]`, NOT via the deprecated bare `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`. A test or CI assertion\n      pins that the override changes provider/model and leaves the rest of the discovered config intact.\n- [ ] An arm whose credential is ABSENT skips LOUDLY and is distinguishable in the run output from an arm\n      that ran and passed — a missing secret must never read as green.\n- [ ] The Bedrock arm authenticates via the AWS credential chain (not an API key), reusing the S7 access\n      precedent; a test or CI assertion pins that it does not silently fall back to `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`.\n- [ ] The per-trigger COST of the matrix is measured and recorded, and the cadence decision (every\n      trigger vs reduced cadence/subset per provider) is stated with its rationale.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass by exit code; the default selection is green; the config-read\n      and env-drift gates pass.\n","parent_id":"061c-ecd1-8967-4a76"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785771467468637001,"uuid":"44f23356-d22c-4432-9606-ecc7da63bcbc"}